Overall project consists of a series of subprojects:
1) ESToolkit: Elysian Shadows Toolkit on all Platforms
2) ElysianVMU: VMU emulator
3) Spine: Runtime library for Skeletal animations
4) OOLua: Lua/C++ binding library
5) Lua5_2: Lua interpreter library
6) UnitTests: Tests for ESToolkit project
7) libGyro: Cross-platform multimedia C API used by ESTk/EVMU

All sub-projects contain Qt .pro files which are converted into Makefiles by qmake. Either use qmake directly from the command-line or build with Qt Creator. 

The MAIN project file is located in qtcreator/ESDev/ESDev.pro. That's the only file you should open in QtCreator, and it'll load all subprojects.

Here are the directions for building the projects from the QtCreator IDE. To build them from the command-line, just look at the Jenkins jobs on the build-server. It's compiling them through the bash shell via SSH.

==================
BUILDING ESTOOLKIT
==================

Building:
Open qtcreator/ESDev/ESDev.pro. On the left, click on "Projects." In the main view, under "Build Steps," add "CONFIG+=estk" where it says "Additional Arguments" to tell the project to build the Toolkit.

Extra:
CONFIG+=tests - Include unit test support in build
CONFIG+=docs - Include Lua API documentation within build

Notes:
ESGamma Folder - Engine-specific code. This code is compiled for both standalone builds and ESTk builds. You'll notice most files have an "elysian_file_base.hpp/cpp" implementation and a "elysian_file_crtp.hpp" wrapper along with a "tk_file.h/cpp" implementation. The crtp header is a wrapper providing a compile-time polymorphic mechanism for engine objects to be compiled as Toolkit-specific subclasses when using ESTk builds. This lets us add additional Qt-specific data and functionality to Toolkit builds without modifying the base engine implementation or incurring additional overhead when the engine is compiled standalone.

AssetIO Folder - This is basically all "asset" objects that are saved and loaded by the Engine and Toolkit. This abstraction was created way back when the Engine and Toolkit were two completely different applications and isn't as important nowadays--although if we release ESTk as a general-purpose Toolkit, AssetIO would be the client library to be compiled within other engines to load ESTk assets, so it's still a useful distinction.

===================
BUILDING ELYSIANVMU
===================

Building: 
Open qtcreator/ESDev/ESDev.pro as with ESToolkit. This time add "CONFIG+=vmu" to the "Additional Arguments" text field under "Build Steps" in the "Projects" pane on the left.


=================
RUNNING ESTOOLKIT
=================

1) Normally:
Executing it normally with no command-line arguments will open ESTk as a GUI normally. Go to File->Open Project and specify the "assets" repository directory to load a project.

2) Unit Tests:
Run ESTk from the command line to run unit tests
ESToolkit.exe --project <path_to_asset_repository_directory> --estk -o -,txt

-o flag tells ESTk how to output test results. See qtest documentation for more information.

3) Asset Tests:
Run ESTk from the command-line to run asset verification tests. These tests are iterating through all levels and areas within the asset directory to ensure that everything can be loaded properly and that there are no issues with resources or Lua scripts.

ESToolkit.exe --project $--project <path_to_asset_repository_directory> -o -,txt

